package com.example;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class MaxUniqueSplit {

    public int maxUniqueSplit(String s) {
        int max = 0;
        Set<String> substrings = new HashSet<>();
        for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 1; i--) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                String substr = s.substring(j, i) + s.substring(i, s.length());
                if (!substrings.contains(substr)) {
                    substrings.add(substr);
                    max++;
                }
            }
        }
        return max;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MaxUniqueSplit splitter = new MaxUniqueSplit();
        System.out.println(splitter.maxUniqueSplit("ababccc")); // Output: 5
    }
}
```

In this solution, we use a Set to keep track of the unique substrings instead of a counter. We add each substring to the Set as we find it and break out of the inner loop if the substring is already in the Set. This reduces the number of iterations by avoiding the need to add any duplicates.

